msgid "DroneCAN was formerly known as UAVCAN."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:17
#: 3dff27c9725c4395864958ae29ca2615
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:19
#: 1941f02b45bf4a65b4d631ed9b815d6b
msgid "A Controller Area Network (CAN bus) is a robust vehicle bus standard designed to allow microcontrollers and devices to communicate with each other in applications without a host computer. It is a message-based protocol, designed originally for multiplex electrical wiring within automobiles to save on copper, but is also used in many other contexts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:25
#: 7420fce860354d9786409042cd73054a
msgid "All nodes are connected to each other through a two wire bus. The wires are 120 Ω nominal twisted pair."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:28
#: e7b2b5c22da047b386e26e9a4cbc0973
msgid "Most autopilots that run ArduPilot have either one or two CAN interfaces for connection of different devices. ArduPilot can support up to 3 CAN interfaces. The setup of the interfaces can be made in a way that will provide redundancy or maximum throughput or a mix of both. This is accomplished with a three layer approach, where apart from the physical interface there exists a driver layer that represents a specific protocol and a software layer (ArduPilot) that communicates on CAN bus through these drivers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:36
#: 99ac2ca9b47f433aa411b7a7a60c26f7
msgid "Each physical interface can be virtually connected to one of up to three drivers that represent the protocols to be used. For example, the most common scenario will be with all interfaces connected to a DroneCAN driver. Such setup will provide redundancy for devices with up to three CAN interfaces and full functionality for devices with one CAN interface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:43
#: 697baaec31214ba4b4e72bbc689d2464
msgid "Configuration settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:46
#: d1461f3c114c4200964e6fd429a59e19
msgid "Enabling CAN interfaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:48
#: 9a60c98793634c6a989a5855ecce0bb0
msgid "Each physical port can be turned off or connected to corresponding driver with parameter ``CAN_Px_DRIVER``, where x is the number of the CAN port. The value of this parameter is the id of driver that will be associated with this port (interface)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:53
#: 3f3e1707905840159f6b3689fb0a8a48
msgid "Each enabled bus/driver will use a block of RAM memory (not Flash) depending on the type of driver and if CANFD is enabled. For example, DroneCAN will allocate 12KB for its driver (24K if CANFD) by default, but can vary from board to board depending on its defaults, if set by its hardware definition file. The ``CAN_Dx_UC_POOL`` parameter can be used to change the pool size. Required pool size depends on bus traffic required by the attached DroneCAN peripheral and can sometimes be reduced for peripherals such as GPS or Compass, whereas peripherals such as ESCs require more bus traffic and therefore a larger pool size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:55
#: 49ad450a0cd74e89b8094bef2d106e71
msgid "For example, the most common setup will have one driver and all interfaces will be connected to it. The :ref:`CAN_P1_DRIVER<CAN_P1_DRIVER>` and :ref:`CAN_P2_DRIVER<CAN_P2_DRIVER>` parameters in this configuration should be set to 1 (first driver). And that driver ( :ref:`CAN_D1_PROTOCOL<CAN_D1_PROTOCOL>`) be set to 1 (DroneCAN)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-canbus-setup-advanced.rst:63
#: a31cd149f6254fe2b91166495f443d61
msgid "After change of any ``CAN_Px_DRIVER`` or ``CAN_Dx_PROTOCOL`` the autopilot has to be rebooted for the changes to take place."
msgstr ""

